What are Optical Frequency Combs?
Optical Frequency Combs (OFCs) are specialized laser sources that generate a spectrum of equally spaced, sharp spectral lines, much like the teeth of a comb. These devices enable ultra-precise measurement of frequency and time by bridging optical and microwave frequencies. Initially developed for Nobel Prize-winning research in optical atomic clocks, OFCs have evolved into critical tools for applications ranging from high-speed data transmission and LIDAR to molecular spectroscopy and environmental monitoring.

OFCs function by producing a stable series of phase-coherent optical frequencies, allowing unparalleled accuracy in applications such as frequency synthesis, optical sampling, and distance ranging. Their ability to deliver absolute frequency references has positioned them as foundational technologies in next-generation scientific, industrial, and communication systems.

